      "text": "The CFTC on 11 August invoked its emergency authority under Section 8a(9) of the Commodity Exchange Act to order KalshiEX to continue operating nationwide, including in New York, after Kalshi petitioned the agency for relief. The petition followed a lawsuit filed on 31 July by New York Attorney General Letitia James and Governor Kathy Hochul seeking $36bn in disgorgement, fines and restitution plus an injunction shutting the platform down; the state alleges Kalshi 'has failed to obtain a license from the New York State Gaming Commission, sidestepping its obligation to pay taxes like licensed casinos and mobile sports gambling platforms do.' CFTC Chairman Mike Selig said 'Congress did not intend for derivatives exchanges to be regulated under a patchwork of state gaming laws', adding that 'New York intends to make event contract derivatives waste away under its iron curtain of state gaming laws before the courts get the chance to issue final rulings' and that 'New York has no business regulating these interstate financial markets.' Kalshi has moved the case to federal court and New York has moved to remand, with both motions pending. This is the CFTC's second such intervention after Michigan, where Kalshi's head of enforcement Robert Denault confirmed the company had already 'unwound the trades' a court ordered. Indian Gaming Association executive director Jason Giles called the agency's conduct 'unprecedented... It just shows they are grasping at straws.'",
