      "title": "Flutter concedes FanDuel Predicts is behind schedule, and the reason is platform, not appetite",
      "text": "At an Oppenheimer fireside chat on 11 August, Flutter CFO Rob Coldrake admitted the FanDuel Predicts rollout has lagged: 'The product rollout has been slightly slower than we would have liked, and we've had some challenges along the way.' He said moving FanDuel's sports and novelty event contracts to Crypto.com will leave the company 'far better positioned' and that Flutter has 'already recently seen quite a significant increase in our volumes.' Asked what share Flutter targets, outgoing CEO Peter Jackson replied 'Large. More than we have now', with Coldrake citing combos expertise as the edge. Eilers & Krejcik told SBC Americas that FanDuel currently holds a low single-digit share of prediction market activity but that sportsbook-turned-prediction operators should gain share sharply in Q3 and Q4. Coldrake said Flutter will assess where Predicts stands by year-end before incoming CEO Dan Taylor decides on 2027 investment, framing H2 spend as 'about focusing on the longer-term U.S. opportunity... That's a lot more important than maximizing short-term EBITDA', and suggested a swing of roughly $200m in EBITDA performance is 'very achievable' by year-end. The lesson for platform vendors: the market leader with the deepest engineering budget in US betting has publicly struggled to bolt order matching, contract settlement and event-contract-specific risk onto a sportsbook stack. That is an argument for pre-integrated exchange and event-contract modules sitting on a shared wallet and single customer view, rather than a parallel product with its own identity and balance.",
