      "title": "Pagcor's minimum guaranteed fees rewrite the unit economics of every Philippine platform deal",
      "text": "Pagcor confirmed to GGRAsia that its minimum guaranteed fee regime for all accredited gaming system administrators took effect on 1 July 2026, having originally been slated for 1 April then deferred twice. Under the first tranche running to 31 December 2026, GSAs offering electronic casino games pay PHP9m (US$147,320) a month once monthly GGR reaches PHP30m, while GSAs without electronic casino games pay PHP3m once GGR reaches PHP15m. From 1 January 2027 those rise to PHP10.5m against a PHP35m threshold and PHP4m against PHP20m. Pagcor said the framework was intended to 'address the gaps in the current fee structure and uphold the principles of fairness, accountability, and fiscal responsibility'. PhilWeb president Brian Ng told GGRAsia in April that the MGF could drive consolidation but would favour 'established and compliant operators', estimating that of 65 active GSAs then, '15 to 20 are contributing to 80 percent of total GGR'; Pagcor's most recent list showed 60 GSAs as of 30 July. For a platform vendor this is a hard commercial constraint rather than a compliance footnote: a fixed monthly floor makes pure revenue-share arrangements on thin-GGR brands loss-making, and it demands per-brand GGR reporting against the PHP15m and PHP30m thresholds in near real time so both parties can see when the floor bites.",
