      "title": "The 40% UK duty has changed what operators buy, not just how much they spend",
      "text": "An Edge Marketing Institute analysis published by iGB on 11 August examines how the doubling of UK Remote Gaming Duty from 21% to 40% in April 2026 - announced in the autumn 2025 budget and described as the largest single tax increase for UK online gambling in history - is reshaping B2B supplier selection. The piece states two operators have already withdrawn from the UK market while larger groups have reported projected additional costs running into nine figures. Its central argument is that the material change is not smaller budgets but different evaluation criteria: procurement conversations now centre on whether an investment increases player lifetime value, reduces churn, improves acquisition efficiency, increases conversion, reduces operational cost or improves retention, and how quickly returns appear. It notes operators are already reviewing promotional spend, affiliate commissions and product mix, cites BDO commentary that operators are using the RGD hike to re-evaluate their cost base and consider M&A, and highlights that the tax change compounds tighter promotional rules including the 10x wagering cap and restrictions on mixed-product promotions. The authors conclude that 'the days of selling innovation for innovation's sake are becoming harder to sustain' and that the suppliers most at risk are not the most expensive but those unable to articulate why continued investment is commercially necessary. Practically, that means instrumentation - attribution, cohort retention, bonus efficiency - is now part of the product, not the sales deck.",
