    "meta": "iGaming Business | 10 Aug 2026",
    "summary": "Scott Longley unpacks a Jefferies deep dive arguing Lottomatica's Italian online leadership is genuinely contestable, putting Lottomatica at 30% of Italian online GGR in Q1 against Flutter's combined Sisal and SNAI position of 27% - with the gap closable simply by recovering the share SNAI has lost.",
    "why": "Most coverage of Italy frames Flutter's position as needing new growth. Jefferies' framing is narrower and more testable: the upside is recovery of ground already lost at one brand, which turns an acquisition thesis into a platform migration execution question.",
    "expanded": "The precedent Jefferies leans on is Sisal: since Flutter bought it in August 2022 its online GGR share has risen from around 10% to 13%, roughly three percentage points gained in both online sports betting and iGaming, and Sisal has outgrown Lottomatica's online business in seven of the past eight quarters and beaten it on iGaming growth in all eight. SNAI has gone the other way, losing around four percentage points of online share, so recovering that lost ground alone could push Flutter past Lottomatica. Jefferies wrote that 'Flutter boasts a track record of gaining the leading market share in almost every targeted territory.' The prize is large: Jefferies estimates 2025 Italian gambling GGR at €22.6bn, Europe's biggest, with online penetration still only 28% against 61% in the UK, and forecasts a 9% online GGR CAGR from 2025 to 2030 - while the new concession regime cuts online licences from 81 to 52 and ad restrictions favour omnichannel incumbents. Jefferies' data through June still showed SNAI's sports betting and iGaming shares declining with 'no material sign' of an inflection, flagging the April platform migration as the catalyst; Peter Jackson countered on the Q2 call that after a 'brief period of share loss' performance 'recovered strongly in June', with average monthly players up 30%.",
    "commentary": "The Jefferies case rests on treating the April SNAI platform migration as a temporary drag that reverses once integration completes. That is the optimistic reading of a migration, and it is worth being sceptical: share loss during a platform move is normal, but share loss that persists past the cutover usually means customers found something they preferred elsewhere rather than something that was briefly broken. One month of 30% player growth is not evidence the decline has reversed, and Lottomatica is not standing still - online revenue up 24% in Q2 and an online adjusted EBITDA margin of 58% in H1, with CEO Guglielmo Angelozzi insisting 'The point is not only acquiring market share, but acquiring quality market share at a sustainable cost.'",
